<article class="env-exercise" id="00GB">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00GB">Exercise <span data-tag="00GB">9.4.1</span>.</a> Let $A$ be an algebra, and let $M$ be a finite-dimensional $A$-bimodule. Recall that in this case, the dual vector space $M^*$ also has a canonical structure of $A$-bimodule, see Exercise&#160;<a href="/tag/003J" data-tag="003J">5.2.1</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Show that we have canonical isomorphisms $(M^*)_ A\simeq (M^ A)^*$, $(M^*)^ A\simeq (M^ A)_*$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Assume in addition that $A$ is a symmetric Frobenius algebra. Show that the following formula defines a non-degenerate bilinear pairing 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  z(A)\otimes A/[A,A]& \to \mathbf{k}\\  z\otimes [a]& \mapsto \varepsilon (za). \end{align*}
</div>
<p>
 Deduce from this that the following formula defines a non-degenerate symmetric bilinear form on $A/[A,A]$: 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  ([a],[b])=\varepsilon (\sum ax_ ibx^ i)  \]
</div>
<p>
 where $x_ i, x^ i$ are dual bases in $A$ with respect to Frobenius pairing. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
Show that if $A$ is a symmetric Frobenius algebra, then $z(A)$ is automatically a commutative Frobenius algebra with the same counit. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>